That's the architecture closed. Nothing left in it that I'd argue with.

Three things to carry into the spec so they don't evaporate between the email and the document:

Write the guardrail down. "Direction always governs" is agreed, but the enforcement clause is the part that matters: under-specification is never grounds for declining the axis. Warmer never compiles colder. Otherwise the conservative default eventually gets read as licence to round the magnitude down to zero, and you're back to shipping the baseline.

Give "conservative" an operational value. By your own rendering rule it can't stay an adjective. Something like: one legible increment from the baseline — a change the user would notice unprompted in a side-by-side, and no more. Repeat gestures step again; "warmer" twice means two steps, not one large one. That's testable in a way "conservative" isn't.

Make the register sample comparative when a baseline exists. Magnitude is only judgable as a delta. A sample shown alone tells the user what the voice is; the same passage rendered baseline-then-adjusted tells them whether the step is the right size — which is the job you've just assigned it. Cheap to specify now, invisible-and-wrong if it isn't.

One residual, minor: multiple gestures on opposing axes — "warmer" plus "more authoritative." Both directions govern; magnitude is where they reconcile. Worth a line in the schema, not a round of discussion.

Otherwise: firewall list next, enumerated per domain, structural entries included, each rendered as a failing check. That's the next artifact, and it's the one that's been waiting longest.
